<header class="start-masthead"><p class="eyebrow">Lab runtime · proposed documentation</p><h1>Prepare local Work in an isolated branch</h1><p>Understand a local proposal, approval, commit and replay without claiming repository admission.</p><p class="source-note">Draft prepared on 2026-10-14. Integration and final T21 qualification remain pending.</p></header>

<section id="runtime-local-work-standing-of-this-journey" aria-labelledby="runtime-local-work-standing-of-this-journey-title"><h2 id="runtime-local-work-standing-of-this-journey-title">Standing of this journey</h2>

<p>The admitted v1 implementation supports authorized local transactions, but this handbook has not replayed a fresh effectful Work journey. Treat this page as the parser-backed preparation and acceptance checklist for that future witness. The <a href="runtime-first-inspection.html">read-only walkthrough</a> is the currently observed tutorial.</p>

<p>Use a new owned branch and a separately selected operator key outside the repository. Obtain explicit authority for that local scope before authorization/application. A local owner string and public key define that branch&#x27;s approval boundary; they do not prove an organizational role or authorize a repository Decision.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-local-work-select-the-inputs-before-creating-anything" aria-labelledby="runtime-local-work-select-the-inputs-before-creating-anything-title"><h2 id="runtime-local-work-select-the-inputs-before-creating-anything-title">Select the inputs before creating anything</h2>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Required decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Runtime/profile</td><td>Choose exact v1/D74 or a separately reviewed v2/D75 candidate; never rebase an existing store</td></tr><tr><td>Store path and ID</td><td>New exclusive path, selected local scope and current HEAD</td></tr><tr><td>Operator identity and key</td><td>Actual independently provisioned local approver, private bytes outside Git</td></tr><tr><td>Work proposal</td><td>Exact inputs, planned outputs, carrier, version, scope and expected source snapshot</td></tr><tr><td>Approval</td><td>Complete proposal/Decision cores/time/owner/store/policy/challenge/HEAD binding</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<p>The <a href="research/sources/implementation/lab-runtime/v1/lab_runtime/cli.py">example subcommand</a> produces planned candidate Work, not a completed task. The <a href="research/sources/implementation/lab-runtime/v1/CONTRACT.md">contract</a> defines the fields that review must assess.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-local-work-follow-the-original-transaction-boundary" aria-labelledby="runtime-local-work-follow-the-original-transaction-boundary-title"><h2 id="runtime-local-work-follow-the-original-transaction-boundary-title">Follow the original transaction boundary</h2>

<p>The parser route is keygen, init, example, review, authorize, apply, then verify/status. Global --store selects the owned branch for every command. keygen rejects private paths inside the repository; init refuses an existing store. review calculates proposed consequences without approving them; authorize signs exact bytes; apply verifies that signature and current HEAD before commit.</p>

<p>One supported transaction can carry independent targets and Decision cores. It records local histories and consequences together. Its receipt and local Project State identify their store scope. A later repository adoption or activated service needs a separate exact governing decision.</p>

<p>Do not use placeholders as working credentials or treat the command names as permission. Have the exact path/identity/body selected and reviewed before execution. The complete command grammar remains in <a href="research/sources/implementation/lab-runtime/v1/README.md">the preserved v1 operator guide</a>.</p>

<figure id="review-commit"><pre><code class="language-mermaid">flowchart LR
  P[Exact candidate proposal] --&gt; R[Review consequences]
  R --&gt; A[Local owner&#x27;s exact signed approval]
  A --&gt; T[Validated staged transaction]
  T --&gt; H[Atomic HEAD commit]
  H --&gt; C[Cold replay and receipt]
  H -. separate .-&gt; D[Derived graph or files]
  R -. different authority .-&gt; O[Repository owner decision]</code></pre><figcaption>An exact local proposal is reviewed, independently authorized, staged and committed at HEAD. Cold replay checks the committed chain. Derived outputs and repository admission have separate boundaries.</figcaption><p>Explanatory component/sequence map, not a declaration of new ontology relation kinds or qualified runtime execution.</p></figure>

</section>

<section id="runtime-local-work-observe-completion-and-residue" aria-labelledby="runtime-local-work-observe-completion-and-residue-title"><h2 id="runtime-local-work-observe-completion-and-residue-title">Observe completion and residue</h2>

<p>For a future witness, retain the actual proposal/approval hashes, expected and resulting HEAD, independent Decision cores, terminal, cold replay and current-source checks. Record whether Work remains planned, was performed, or has a separately assessed outcome. A successful local admission receipt is not Work satisfaction.</p>

<p>If interrupted, inspect the owned store before deleting anything. Committed history survives; recognized uncommitted staging may be reconciled under expected HEAD. Unknown residue is retained. Do not recycle a proposal ID with different material, or overwrite earlier evidence after a failure.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-local-work-review-and-cleanup-criteria" aria-labelledby="runtime-local-work-review-and-cleanup-criteria-title"><h2 id="runtime-local-work-review-and-cleanup-criteria-title">Review and cleanup criteria</h2>

<p>The tutorial is eligible for a successful walkthrough only after a fresh isolated execution, exact authority selection, receipt, replay/recovery and cleanup have been observed. Dispose only its own known disposable roots/keys under the accepted retention policy; preserve committed public evidence and any uncertainty. Link <a href="runtime-recovery.html">recovery</a> and <a href="lab-reference-library.html#def-cal-definition-work">the canonical Work definition</a> when reviewing the result.</p>

</section>

